# =============================================================================
# torrc template — rendered by entrypoint.sh at container start
# =============================================================================
# __HASHED_PASSWORD__ is substituted with the output of `tor --hash-password`
# so the control password never has to be stored hashed in the repository.
# =============================================================================

# Substituted with `User tor` when the entrypoint runs as root (docker-compose),
# and with a comment when it already runs as an unprivileged uid (Kubernetes),
# where there is no user to switch to.
__USER_DIRECTIVE__

DataDirectory /var/lib/tor/data

# SOCKS proxy. Used by the backend's loopback self-check, which fetches our own
# .onion through Tor to prove the descriptor is published and the service
# answers. Bound to the container network only — never published to the host.
SocksPort 0.0.0.0:9050

# An open SOCKS proxy is usable by anything that shares this network, so
# restrict it explicitly rather than relying on topology alone. These are the
# RFC1918 ranges container networks draw from; narrow them to the exact
# backend subnet if it is known. The trailing reject is what makes this an
# allow-list — without it Tor's default would accept everything.
#
# It may also need WIDENING, and the symptom is easy to misread: a cluster that
# allocates pod addresses outside these ranges (100.64.0.0/10 on some managed
# node pools, or an IPv6-only pod network) has its backend rejected HERE, the
# loopback self-check then reports the onion unreachable, and Dark Protocol
# reports Unavailable on a deployment whose onion service is in fact working.
# Add the cluster's pod CIDR if that is what you are seeing.
SocksPolicy accept 10.0.0.0/8
SocksPolicy accept 172.16.0.0/12
SocksPolicy accept 192.168.0.0/16
SocksPolicy reject *

# Control port. The backend uses it read-only: bootstrap progress, circuit
# state, hidden-service configuration. Password-authenticated.
ControlPort 0.0.0.0:9051
HashedControlPassword __HASHED_PASSWORD__

# The v3 onion service. Its private key lives in this directory, which is a
# volume private to this container — the backend is never given access to it;
# it only receives a copy of the public `hostname` file.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/hidden_service
HiddenServiceVersion 3

# Forward to the dedicated onion ingress listener, NOT the clearnet backend
# port. That separation is what lets the backend tell an anonymous request from
# a clearnet one: the ingress port is reachable only from this container, so
# arriving on it is a network fact a remote client cannot fake.
#
# __ONION_TARGET__ is an IP:port by the time it lands here -- entrypoint.sh
# resolves it, because this directive does not accept a hostname (Tor parses
# the target as an address or a unix socket, never a DNS name).
HiddenServicePort 80 __ONION_TARGET__

Log notice stdout
